Modern vehicles depend on an expanding network of electronic control units, sensors, processors, connectivity modules and power-conversion systems. Automotive power management integrated circuits (PMICs) regulate and distribute power to these electronic subsystems, helping maintain stable operating voltages, manage power sequencing and monitor electrical conditions. As vehicles adopt more advanced driver-assistance systems, digital cockpits and electrified powertrains, power management becomes increasingly important to system reliability and integration.

